// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// property: the doors, and which building each one belongs to
//
// Settled with Patrick on 21 September: every building in the game is claimable,
// claiming costs nothing and happens at the door, it hands you the deed there and
// then, a home and its farm are one claim, you may hold more than one, and a
// claim lapses after two weeks.
//
// These two tables are what the server needs to answer any of that, and they are
// generated by tools\Build-DoorTable.py, which walks every placed reference in
// all 101 plugins. The measurement behind them: 5,658 placed doors, 2,798 of
// them leading somewhere, and 852 interior cells reached through one, which is
// the number of claimable buildings this world actually holds. 425 of those have
// more than one way in, which is why a claim covers a BUILDING and never a
// doorway, and why a lock has to follow the claim rather than the door somebody
// happened to be standing at.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/* Placed door -> the building it belongs to. Written by
   scripts\dev\Build-DoorTable.py into its table file. The far side when the far
   side is an interior, which is the door you walk in through, otherwise its
   own cell when that is one. Both halves of a front door answer with the
   house, which is what lets a lock work from either side of it. */
const BUILDING_BY_DOOR = table('BUILDING_BY_DOOR');

/* Building -> what people call it. Written by scripts\dev\Build-DoorTable.py
   into its table file. Read out of the cell's FULL, which in a localised
   plugin is a string id into a .STRINGS table inside a BSA. A deed says
   this, so an editor id here is an editor id in somebody's pack. */
const BUILDING_NAME = table('BUILDING_NAME');

/* Placed door -> 1 when it is a way out rather than a way in. Written
   by scripts\dev\Build-DoorTable.py into its table file. A door that stands
   inside the building it belongs to, or in a room whose only road to open
   ground is through that building. A lock keeps people out and never
   shuts them in, so none of these is ever locked. */
const WAY_OUT = table('WAY_OUT');

/*
  TOWN GATES ARE CLAIMABLE PROPERTIES (#615).

  Patrick, 27 September: city gates SHOULD be claimable via the key mold flow.
  Build-DoorTable only maps doors that open into an interior cell, so a town
  gate (exterior leaf to exterior leaf) was never a building. Opening the
  Whiterun gate and saying /claim then fell through to farming, which answered
  "Only a farmer can bring a crop in."

  These four leaves are the town gates (Thornswood-Last.esp loads the outer ones
  unlocked since #2484). The inside
  Whiterun leaf is a way out so a lock never shuts people inside the city.
  Riften's two listed leaves are both the locked outer ones from the same
  measurement that put them in GATES in scripts/mods/Build-LastPlugin.py.
*/
(function addTownGateProperties() {
  var doors = {
    '1b1f1:Skyrim.esm': 'gate:whiterun-main',
    '1b1f3:Skyrim.esm': 'gate:whiterun-main',
    '42284:Skyrim.esm': 'gate:riften-main',
    '42286:Skyrim.esm': 'gate:riften-main'
  };
  var names = {
    'gate:whiterun-main': 'Whiterun Main Gate',
    'gate:riften-main': 'Riften Main Gate'
  };
  var outs = { '1b1f3:Skyrim.esm': 1 };
  for (var d in doors) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(doors, d)) { BUILDING_BY_DOOR[d] = doors[d]; }
  }
  for (var b in names) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(names, b)) { BUILDING_NAME[b] = names[b]; }
  }
  for (var w in outs) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(outs, w)) { WAY_OUT[w] = outs[w]; }
  }
})();


